          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            Sensible reasoning in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            in general, and particularly in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            under the respective notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} presented
            by other structures, since the notion of equivalence of the presentation can be finer than that of the notion being presented.
          %p
            For instance, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            can be presented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of a corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict category
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "nerve#NerveOfACategory"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of simplicial sets is much finer than
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalences
            of their corresponding categories. It is generally a
            %em mistake
            to mix up these two levels and, for instance, assign to a category properties that are shared only by
            %em some
            of the simplicial sets representing it, say, by distinguishing between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed "." do
              %em breaks the equivalence invariance
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Breaking"} below
          %p The ideas here generalize in many directions. For example, not only properties, but also constructions involving categories and functors, can fail to preserve equivalences.
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
                proposed the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Principle of Isomorphism
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/lecture-notes-in-logic/Logic%20Colloquium%20'95:%20Proceedings%20of%20the%20Annual%20European%20Summer%20Meeting%20of%20the%20Association%20of%20Symbolic%20Logic,%20held%20in%20Haifa,%20Israel,%20August%209-18,%201995/chapter/Towards%20a%20Categorical%20Foundation%20of%20Mathematics/lnl/1235415906"} paper
              %p “Towards a categorical foundation of mathematics”, in Johann A. Makowsky, Elena V. Ravve, eds., Logic Colloquium ‘95: Proceedings of the Annual European Summer Meeting of the Association of Symbolic Logic, held in Haifa, Israel, August 9-18, 1995 (Berlin: Springer-Verlag, 1998), 153-190.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Peter Aczel
                mentioned a similar concept, the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} Structure Identity Principle
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mfo.de/document/1145/OWR_2011_52.pdf"} report 52/2011
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"} me
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %li
              %p
                A very precise way of stating this idea is encapsulated in
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
                for mathematics. By identifying equivalences/isomorphisms with inhabitants of an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                are invariant under such (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#AhrensNorth18"} AhrensNorth18
            %li#evil
              %p
                In the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
                literature, a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definition"} definition
                has been called “evil” if it breaks equivalence invariance (see e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Pavlovic23"} Pavlovic 2023
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#FerrerEtAl24"} Ferrer et al. 2024
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/street/street2.pdf#page=4"} p. 4 here
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/corfield/2006/08/klein-2-geometry-iv.html#115517983940912832"} here
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “But what do I mean by
                  = succeed "?" do
                    %strong evil
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %h3#from_mathematical_practice From mathematical practice
          %p …
          %blockquote
            %p
              Draw an analogy with vector spaces (maybe just finite-dimensional ones?). We can
              %em use
              a basis to study a vector space, but the basis is not
              %em part of
              the vector space. Even if we use a foundation of mathematics in which the basis literally is part of the vector space (which arguably was the case for 18th-century linear algebra, which studied only Cartesian spaces; and is still the case today if we study finite-dimensional vector spaces using a foundational type theory with propositions as types, but probably this is too obscure), anything that we consider to be a property
              %em of the vector space
              should be independent of the basis. We can make this precise by comparing the groupoids
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Vect
                      %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect_\sim
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect_b
          %p …
          %h3#from_foundations_and_logic From foundations and logic
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
            and can be compared for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+pair"} ordered pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0)
            is equal to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            by some standards and equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            by other standards (while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,1) = 2
            by yet other standards), but this makes no difference mathematically. In contrast,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            either implicitly (as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS+with+elements"} ETCS with elements
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            of a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relations"} binary relations
            between two given sets, etc). This can be built into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            (which can serve as a foundation all by itself). The result is that, depending on precisely how one writes definitions, it may or may not be possible to compare two things for equality. For example, the ordered pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1, 1)
            is equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            under triangular encoding, equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            under binary encoding, and equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            under Chinese Remainder encoding. But if we take ordered pairs as primitive, it makes no sense to compare
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1, 1)
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            Does it make sense, then, to ask whether two arbitrary
            %em sets
            are equal? In material set theory, of course it does; two sets are equal if they have the same elements (by the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            with equality automatically gives this question (whether two arbitrary sets are equal) a meaning, as do the most common forms of foundational type theory, but this is again never used in mathematics. (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR
            , which is based on first-order logic without equality, does not give this question a meaning and does not suffer for it.) So just as we don't need to ask whether
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0) = 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(0,0)\} = \{2\}
            as sets. (Note that this is a completely different question, structurally, from whether, say,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(0,0)\} = \{2\}
            as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \coprod (\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N})
          %p
            Now let us move from the foundations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            to those of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of a category don't form a set, then this is only because of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/size+issues"} size issues
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            is itself an example of a category, then by the previous paragraph, it does
            %em not
            have to make sense (and is mathematically meaningless) to test the objects of this category for equality. So we get the idea that we cannot compare objects of a given category for equality in general (although this may make sense for particular categories). In other words, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is not by default a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict category
            (a category equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+stuff"} extra stuff
            of an explicit equality predicate on its objects).
          %p
            We can found mathematics on type theory without identity types, and then it is literally true that (in general) it makes no sense to compare objects of a given type for equality; one has to
            %em define
            a relevant equality predicate when there is one. From the other direction, we can found mathematics on (weak)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} infinity-groupoid
            theory, i.e.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (as in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            However, these are not commonly used foundations. Therefore, category theory is usually written in a language in which it does literally make sense to ask whether two objects of a given category are equal (meaning something stronger than that they are isomorphic), whether two functors between two given categories are equal, etc. If we want these definitions to make sense in the general mathematical situation (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is an example of a category, even though comparing two arbitrary sets for equality makes no mathematical sense), then we need to check that our definitions are compatible with the principle of equivalence.
          %p
            (Strictly speaking, it does not even make sense to ask if two arbitrary sets are isomorphic; going back to our earlier example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(0,0)\}
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{2\}
            as objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            because they are both singletons, but they are not isomorphic as objects of the poset of subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo +
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} + \mathbb{N}\times \mathbb{N}
          %h3#from_philosophy From philosophy
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tractatus+Logico-Philosophicus"} Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wittgenstein"} Wittgenstein
            writes
          %blockquote
            %p Either a thing has properties that nothing else has, in which case we can immediately use a description to distinguish it from the others and refer to it; or, on the other hand, there are several things that have the whole set of their properties in common, in which case it is quite impossible to indicate one of them. For if there is nothing to distinguish a thing, I cannot distinguish it, since otherwise it would be distinguished after all.
          %p
            Given that a main theme of the Tractatus is on the limits of language, it is tempting to consider this as a description not just for ‘the world’ (as in
            %em Tractatus
            1.), but for any given language and the objects about which one reasons in that language. This seems to encapsulate Makkai’s motivation alluded to above.
          %h2#general_definition General definition
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %em compatible with equivalence
            if, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
            holds for an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
            (as an object of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(b)
            holds. Alternatively, an operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to objects of (another
            = succeed "-groupoid)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is
            %strong compatible with equivalence
            if, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are equivalent objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(b)
            are equivalent objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %p
            (Note that everything is an object of some
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-morphism" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} underlying
            = succeed "-groupoid," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-morphism" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-arrow" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The operation-version of the principle of equivalence gives the property-version if you think of a property as an operation taking values in the
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-groupoid," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth values
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            (’
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-functor’, if you prefer). A property adheres to the principle of equivalence precisely if it's a functor to the
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            This definition should be the conclusion of a theorem that using certain language (including avoiding equations between objects of a category) makes it impossible to say anything that violates the principle of equivalence.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
            works on such a language,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FOLDS"} FOLDS
            (‘first-order logic with dependent sorts’), which does not include an axiomatic notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            at all. This pertains to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "foundations#MFCT"} mathematical foundations of category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/makkai/"} First Order Logic with Dependent Sorts, with Applications to Category Theory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#in_category_theory In category theory
          %h4#strict_concrete_categories Strict concrete categories
          %p
            A common misconception in category theory is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            violate the principle of equivalence. However, this is not true. We have to first make sure that the objects of the categories are “sets” in a suitable sense, yielding the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
            be some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
            is strict or not; e.g. in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
            is a general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/precategory"} precategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
            is not a strict category, the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic+with+equality"} first order logic with equality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            in the sense of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-proposition"} h-proposition
            valued
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unique+identification"} unique identification
            of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
          %p
            A category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            if it comes with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C \to \mathrm{Set}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
          %p
            Another common misconception in category theory is that all strict concrete categories violate the principle of equivalence. However, this is also not true. There are some strict concrete categories in which the equivalence of objects are precisely equality on objects: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+category"} gaunt
            concrete categories. One example of a guant concrete category is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphisms"} ring homomorphisms
          %p
            Instead, what does violate the principle of equivalence is any strict concrete category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            which is not a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+category"} gaunt category
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/families+of+sets"} families of sets
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+set"} pointed set
              with designated element
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}:V \to \mathrm{Set}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+sets"} family of sets
              indexed by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in \mathrm{El}(A)
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \neq y
            %p
              Then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo ,
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{V,\mathrm{El}}
              whose objects are elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              and whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              from sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}(a)
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}(b)
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in V
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
              that breaks equivalence invariance, since
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              does not coincide with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              of the family
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in V
              and the two distinct elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in \mathrm{El}(A)
              are required to make the resulting category not a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+category"} gaunt category
          %p
            The equivalence-invariant version is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+completion"} Rezk completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{C}_{V,\mathrm{El}}
            of this category, yielding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+category"} univalent category
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \mathrm{El})
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+sets"} small sets
          %p
            Specific examples of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \mathrm{El})
            which generate such strict concrete categories of sets include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}
                is the family of standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi k
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}(n) \coloneqq \{k \in \mathbb{N} \vert k \lt n\}
            %li
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} extensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membership+relation"} membership relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi V
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in_V y
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}
                is defined as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≔
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi V
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{El}(a) \coloneqq \{x \in V \vert x \in_V a\}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
                and
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Peter Aczel
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#GGMS24"} Gratzer, Gylterud, Mörtberg and Stenholm 2024
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            done in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+foundations"} set-level foundations
            such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory+with+classes"} set theory with classes
            or a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+type+theory"} set-level type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            on them, which means that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            is strict and every non-gaunt concrete category thus violates the principle of equivalence, including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            itself. As a result, in order to be equivalence invariant, one has two options:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                one can instead say that they are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
                and then (usually) impose some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+relation"} coherence relation
                on the relevant family of isomorphisms. But of course, this is more complicated!
            %li
              %p
                one can work in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-level+foundations"} higher-level foundations
                instead, where the foundational collections do not have propositional valued equality by default, sidestepping this issue entirely.
          %h4#identityonobjects_functors Identity-on-objects functors
          %p
            The concept of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
            appears in category theory as well, particularly when defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+categories"} Freyd categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+categories"} dagger categories
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong
                identity-on-objects
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: A\to B
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is a functor between categories with the same collection of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi ob
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{ob}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
              on the collection of objects.
          %p
            However, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theories"} material set theories
            or material class theories, “having the same collection of objects” is sometimes defined as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo =
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob = ob(A) = ob(B)
          %p
            Instead, what category theorists usually mean by two categories having the same objects is actually having a single
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set \times Set)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g):A(x,y) \times B(x,y))
            for objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (h,k)\circ(f,g) = (h \circ f,k \circ g)
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A(x,y)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:B(x,y)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:A(y,z)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k:B(y,z)
          %p
            Another problem occurs in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theories"} set theories
            with certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+categories"} concrete categories
            like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilb"} Hilb
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+category"} permutation category
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi ob
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_ob
            on the objects is the identity function violates the principle of equality, because by definition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_ob(x) = x
            for all objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            Instead, when category theorists are talking about two categories with the same collection of objects and and identity-on-objects functor, what they really are talking about is an
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^I
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h4#daggers
            In the concept of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-category"} †-category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\dagger: Hom_C(A,B) \to hom_C(B,A)
            for every object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \in Ob(C)
          %ul
            %li
              For every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in Ob(C)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo †
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_A)^\dagger = 1_A
            %li
              For every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \in Ob(C)
              and every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in Hom_C(A,B)
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in Hom_C(B,C)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo †
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo †
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g \circ f)^\dagger = f^\dagger \circ g^\dagger
            %li
              For every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \in Ob(C)
              and every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in Hom_C(A,B)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo †
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo †
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((f)^\dagger)^\dagger = f
          %p
            However, there is another definition of a †-category, as a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C \to C^{op}
          %p
            which is the identity on objects and has
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^2 = 1
          %p This second definition break equivalence-invariance: it imposes equations between objects, so we cannot transport a dagger-category structure along an equivalence of categories.
          %h5#historical_comments Historical comments
          %p It was once believed that there was no known way to express the idea without equations between objects. This stemmed from the fact that category theorists were using functors to define the dagger. It was only later that it was recognized that one could include the dagger in the definition of dagger categories in the same way that one includes composition of morphisms in the definition of category, which resulted in a definition of dagger category that doesn’t violate the principle of equivalence.
          %p
            A discussion about this is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/4201/principle-of-equivalence/?Focus=101442#Comment_101442"} archived on the nForum
          %h4#in_higher_category_theory In higher category theory
          %p
            It violates the principle of equivalence to state that two morphisms in a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} category
            are equal, because these morphisms are objects in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-category"} hom-category
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} category
            %em every
            claim of equality break equivalence-invariance.
          %p
            Defining higher categorial structures using such equalities tends to lead to
            %em strict
            concepts; avoiding them and imposing coherence relations leads to
            %em weak
            concepts. Sometimes there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem"} coherence theorem
            showing that every weak concept can be strictified, which justifies using equality as a figure of speech. See
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-category"} Gray-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            for examples of this in action.
          %h3#in_physics In physics
          %p
            Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilb"} Hilb
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nCob"} nCob
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-categories"} dagger-categories
            %a{:href => "#daggers"} above
            is relevant, particularly for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
          %h4#in_gauge_theory In gauge theory
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"}> gauge transformation
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+equivalence"} gauge equivalence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariance
          %h4#in_gravity In gravity
          %p
            The principle of equivalence in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+principle+%28physics%29"} equivalence principle (physics)
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+transformation"} coordinate transformation
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
          %h2#Breaking How to break equivalence-invariance
          %p
            Just as we can make use of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} bases
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            to discuss
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            (and other well-known categories such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            %em are
            strict categories. Mathematically, strict categories form a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-groupoid"} groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat_\sim
            that is different from the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\sim
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo+functor"} pseudo functor
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat_\sim
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\sim
            that we may find useful.
          %p
            Much as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            tells us that every vector space has a basis, so the global axiom of choice tells us that every category may be given the structure of a strict category. Then we can use this extra structure (in either case), as long as we prove that the result is independent of the structure chosen. Even if we don't wish to accept the axiom of choice, we can still prove a theorem about those vector spaces that have bases or those categories that can be given a strict structure.
          %p
            Perhaps the extreme case of this is using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            to study
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} categories
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoid
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-level+foundations"} higher-level foundations
          %h2#References References
          %p
            A discussion of the principle of equivalence in the very
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of mathematics by replacing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations/formalization of mathematics and homotopy theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://video.ias.edu/node/68"} video
            %li#CoquandDanielsson
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"}> Thierry Coquand
                , Nils Anders Danielsson,
                %em Isomorphism is equality
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cse.chalmers.se/~nad/publications/coquand-danielsson-isomorphism-is-equality.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Awodey14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                ,
                %em Structuralism, Invariance, and Univalence
                (2014) (
                %a{:href => "https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/preprints/siu.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#AhrensNorth18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benedikt+Ahrens"}> Benedikt Ahrens
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paige+Randall+North"} Paige Randall North
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Univalent foundations and the equivalence principle
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://paigenorth.github.io/ep.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The category of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Peter Aczel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+universe"} Tarski universe
            of sets (and thus not equivalence invariant):
          %ul
            %li#GGMS24
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%A5kon+Gylterud"} Håkon Gylterud
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"} Anders Mörtberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elisabeth+Stenholm"} Elisabeth Stenholm
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Category of Iterative Sets in Homotopy Type Theory and Univalent Foundations
              %strong 34
              Special Issue 9:
              %em Advances in Homotopy type theory
              (2024) 945-970 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129524000288"}> doi:10.1017/S0960129524000288
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04893"} arXiv:2402.04893
          %p
            For the use of the term “evil” to refer to the violation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %ul
            %li#JohnsonFreyd23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"}> Theo Johnson-Freyd
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher Dagger Categories
                %a{:href => "Center+for+Quantum+and+Topological+Systems#JohnsonFreydNov2023"} talk at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CQTS"} CQTS
                (08 Nov 2023) &amp;lbrack;slides:
                %a{:href => "http://categorified.net/NYUADtalk.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/JohnsonFreyd-HigherDaggerNYUAD.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/zvtziTpl2T0"} YT
            %li#Pavlovic23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dusko+Pavlovic"}> Dusko Pavlovic
